A1. Tar containers. ~ SHIPS
A5. A point on the compass. ~ SSW
A8. What it takes to get ahead. ~ SPEED
A13. What cattle are found in. ~ HERD
A14. These are higher in Hawaii—and everywhere else. ~ RATES
A16. Magnetic attraction for women. ~ SALE
A17. Before. ~ ERE
A18. You can almost hear this in the White House. ~ SILENCE
A20. Something you've got to hand to a lot of people. ~ TIP
A21. Favorite pronoun of flappers. ~ HE
A22. What grandma used to do to keep busy. ~ TAT
A23. The Speaker of the House. ~ MA
A25. A good place to go since Prohibition. ~ ABROAD
A28. Numeral of a much-married English King. ~ EIGHTH
A31. The attention lovers get from their sweeties. ~ RAPT
A32. Any little Quaker might say this to her boyfriend. ~ YEA
A34. This causes many an exposure. ~ TEAR
A35. Anyone in this condition has nothing on. ~ BARE
A36. What Judge's Lizzie Labels exist on. ~ FORDS
A38. What the Big Business man did to get action. ~ SUED
A40. This runs up and down in N. Y. City. (Abbr.) ~ AVE
A41. A silent pardner. ~ DAD
A42. You must have a sense of direction to do this. ~ AIM
A43. This fellow likes his port. ~ TAR
A44. What the Prohibition agent does ~ NABS
A46. What Satan is supposed to do. ~ TEMPT
A48. What early settlers found in the U. S. A.—and they are still worrying in Washington. ~ REDS
A49. What the fairest flower does when it is plucked. ~ DIES
A51. Many things are more brilliant this way. ~ LIT
A52. This means a lot of pull. ~ HAUL
A53. This isn't so valuable as the spirit it contains. ~ BOTTLE
A54. What Scotchmen are at a Rickard prize fight. ~ BARRED
A56. This is often used as a conjunction. ~ SO
A57. A well-known play Homer should have written. ~ BAT
A59. A much-used pronoun. ~ ME
A60. What you lose every time you stand up. ~ LAP
A63. These showed the way to the K. K. K. ~ TORCHES
A66. A lady you can find in IDAHO. ~ IDA
A68. A bold beast of Africa. ~ LION
A70. What the light that fails should be. ~ RELIT
A71. Soon; straightway. ~ ANON
A72. No wide awake person can do this. ~ SLEEP
A73. Where Horizontal 68 spent his mornings. ~ DEN
A74. A kick in the pants. ~ FLASK

D1. The sheik's lady friend. ~ SHEBA
D2. A pronoun. ~ HER
D3. This causes anger. ~ IRE
D4. This kind of bill causes no worry. (Abbr.) ~ PD
D5. This is often shaken at dinner time. ~ SALT
D6. This smokes whereever it goes. ~ STEAMER
D7. A part of the verb "to go." ~ WENT
D9. An afterthought. (Abbr.) ~ PS
D10. What to do at a filling station. ~ EAT
D11. The old man of New Haven, Conn. ~ ELI
D12. This is deep stuff. ~ DEPTH
D14. A state on the Atlantic Coast. (Abbr.) ~ RI
D15. Another—farther south. (Abbr.) ~ SC
D18. This is valuable when the S. R. O. sign is up. ~ SEAT
D19. To give forth. ~ EMIT
D21. Old maids lose this at 40. ~ HOPE
D24. What the ladies of vertical 21 never give way. ~ AGES
D26. What the bad man of the plains carried on with. ~ BRAVADO
D27. This precedes many a nightmare. ~ RAREBIT
D29. This is haughty—and not so nice. ~ HAUTEUR
D30. The part of a loom moved by the foot. ~ TREADLE
D32. What to do when you miss your Swiss. ~ YODEL
D33. To make to correspond. ~ ADAPT
D35. A prohibition. ~ BAN
D36. These live on the fat of the land. ~ FAT
D37. What Congressmen do most of the time. ~ SIT
D39. People who bury their mistakes. (Abbr.) ~ DRS
D45. What our opponent usually does to us at Bridge. ~ SETS
D47. What should be the name of a play in which one Scotchman licks three Irishmen ? ~ MIRACLE
D48. What are pre-war spirits? ~ RARE
D50. Many a penny is put in this. ~ SLOT
D52. These look enviously at the stars on Broadway. ~ HAMS
D53. What are due on a due, due, duey day? ~ BILLS
D55. What did Socrates do to end it all? ~ DRANK
D57. Dumb Dora thinks this means "the staff of life." ~ BRED
D58. These people don't carry much weight. ~ THIN
D61. To feel pain. ~ AIL
D62. An American poet whose name was the same as a food made by the natives of Hawaii. ~ POE
D64. The little companion of "either." ~ OR
